solution:
[Overview]
This is an algebra problem about roots of quadratic equations. The goal is to evaluate a product involving the roots of two monic quadratics. The key idea is to group the factors involving gamma and delta, then use Vieta's relations and the quadratic equations satisfied by gamma and delta.

[Plan]
P1. Record the Vieta relations for alpha, beta and gamma, delta.
P2. Group the four factors into two products, one involving gamma and one involving delta.
P3. Simplify each grouped product using the quadratic equations satisfied by gamma and delta.
P4. Multiply the simplified products and use gamma delta = 1.

[Solution]
S1. [setup] By Vieta, \alpha+\beta=-p, \alpha\beta=1, \gamma+\delta=-q, and \gamma\delta=1.
S2. [setup] Let E=(\alpha-\gamma)(\beta-\gamma)(\alpha+\delta)(\beta+\delta), and group it as E=((\alpha-\gamma)(\beta-\gamma))((\alpha+\delta)(\beta+\delta)).
S3. [symbolic] Expand the first grouped factor using Vieta -> {{(\alpha-\gamma)(\beta-\gamma) => \gamma^2+p\gamma+1}}
S4. [symbolic] Use \gamma^2+q\gamma+1=0 to simplify -> {{\gamma^2+p\gamma+1 => (p-q)\gamma}}
S5. [symbolic] Expand the second grouped factor using Vieta -> {{(\alpha+\delta)(\beta+\delta) => \delta^2-p\delta+1}}
S6. [symbolic] Use \delta^2+q\delta+1=0 to simplify -> {{\delta^2-p\delta+1 => -(p+q)\delta}}
S7. [symbolic] Substitute the simplified factors into E -> {{E => -(p-q)(p+q)\gamma\delta}}
S8. [symbolic] Use \gamma\delta=1 -> {{E => -(p-q)(p+q)}}
S9. [symbolic] Expand the product -> {{-(p-q)(p+q) => q^2-p^2}}

[Check]
V1. Take p=1 and q=2. Then gamma=delta=-1, while alpha+beta=-1 and alpha beta=1.
V2. Direct calculation gives (\alpha+1)(\beta+1)=1 and (\alpha-1)(\beta-1)=3, so E=3.
V3. The formula gives q^2-p^2=4-1=3, which matches the direct calculation.

[Answer]
\boxed{q^2-p^2}

