solution:
[Overview]
This is an exponential equation problem. The goal is to find all real x satisfying the given rational equation. The key idea is to write the terms using a=2^x and b=3^x, then reduce the equation to a quadratic in the ratio a/b.

[Plan]
P1. Set a=2^x and b=3^x, using positivity of exponential functions.
P2. Rewrite the equation in terms of a and b.
P3. Factor and cancel the common positive factor a+b.
P4. Introduce the positive ratio t=a/b and solve the resulting quadratic.
P5. Translate the values of t back to x.
P6. Verify the solutions in the original equation.

[Solution]
S1. [setup] Let a=2^x and b=3^x. Since exponential functions are positive, a>0 and b>0.
S2. [symbolic] Rewrite the equation in terms of a and b -> {{(8^x+27^x)/(12^x+18^x)=7/6 => (a^3+b^3)/(a^2 b+a b^2)=7/6}}
S3. [symbolic] Factor numerator and denominator -> {{(a^3+b^3)/(a^2 b+a b^2) => ((a+b)(a^2-ab+b^2))/(ab(a+b))}}
S4. [symbolic] Cancel the positive factor a+b -> {{((a+b)(a^2-ab+b^2))/(ab(a+b)) => (a^2-ab+b^2)/(ab)}}
S5. [setup] Define t=a/b=(2/3)^x. Since a>0 and b>0, t>0.
S6. [symbolic] Divide the expression by ab -> {{(a^2-ab+b^2)/(ab)=7/6 => a/b - 1 + b/a = 7/6}}
S7. [symbolic] Substitute t=a/b and 1/t=b/a -> {{a/b - 1 + b/a = 7/6 => t - 1 + 1/t = 7/6}}
S8. [symbolic] Rearrange the equation -> {{t - 1 + 1/t = 7/6 => t + 1/t = 13/6}}
S9. [symbolic] Clear the denominator -> {{t + 1/t = 13/6 => 6t^2-13t+6=0}}
S10. [symbolic] Factor the quadratic -> {{6t^2-13t+6 => (3t-2)(2t-3)}}
S11. [symbolic] Solve the factors -> {{(3t-2)(2t-3)=0 => t=2/3 or t=3/2}}
S12. [symbolic] Replace t by (2/3)^x and solve -> {{(2/3)^x=2/3 or (2/3)^x=3/2 => x=1 or x=-1}}

[Check]
V1. Substitute x=1 into the original expression -> <<(8^1+27^1)/(12^1+18^1)=7/6>>
V2. Substitute x=-1 into the original expression -> <<(8^(-1)+27^(-1))/(12^(-1)+18^(-1))=7/6>>

[Answer]
\boxed{-1, 1}

[Overview]
This is a logarithmic equation problem. The goal is to evaluate a power expression using given base-60 exponential facts. The key idea is to convert the exponents to base-60 logarithms, simplify the exponent, and then evaluate the resulting power.

[Plan]
P1. Take base-60 logarithms of the two given equations.
P2. Simplify the numerator and denominator in the exponent using logarithm laws.
P3. Rewrite the exponent with natural logarithms and reduce it.
P4. Evaluate the final power expression.
P5. Verify the result by reversing the calculation.

[Solution]
S1. [setup] From 60^a = 3 and 60^b = 5, taking logarithms base 60 gives a = log_60(3) and b = log_60(5).
S2. [symbolic] Simplify the numerator of the exponent -> {{1 - a - b => log_60(4)}}
S3. [symbolic] Simplify the denominator factor of the exponent -> {{1 - b => log_60(12)}}
S4. [symbolic] Substitute these logarithms into the exponent -> {{(1 - a - b)/(2(1 - b)) => log_60(4)/(2 log_60(12))}}
S5. [symbolic] Rewrite with natural logarithms and cancel ln(60) -> {{log_60(4)/(2 log_60(12)) => ln(4)/(2 ln(12))}}
S6. [symbolic] Reduce the exponent using ln(4) = 2 ln(2) -> {{ln(4)/(2 ln(12)) => ln(2)/ln(12)}}
S7. [symbolic] Evaluate the original power with this exponent -> {{12^(ln(2)/ln(12)) => 2}}

[Check]
V1. The simplified exponent is ln(2)/ln(12).
V2. Reversing the calculation gives 12^(ln(2)/ln(12)) = 2, so the result is consistent.

[Answer]
\boxed{2}

