solution:
[Overview]
This is a trigonometric simplification problem. The goal is to simplify the product (1 + tan 20 degrees)(1 + tan 25 degrees). The key idea is to use the tangent addition formula and the fact that 20 degrees + 25 degrees = 45 degrees.

[Plan]
P1. Recognize that the two angles add to 45 degrees.
P2. Apply the tangent addition formula to tan(20 degrees + 25 degrees).
P3. Use tan 45 degrees = 1 to derive a relation between tan 20 degrees and tan 25 degrees.
P4. Expand the desired product and substitute the derived relation.

[Solution]
S1. [setup] Let A = 20 degrees and B = 25 degrees, so A + B = 45 degrees.
S2. [theorem] Use the tangent addition formula tan(A + B) = (tan A + tan B)/(1 - tan A tan B).
S3. [derive] Since A + B = 45 degrees, tan(A + B) = tan 45 degrees = 1.
S4. [symbolic] Substitute the value into the tangent addition formula -> {{1 = (tan A + tan B)/(1 - tan A tan B) => tan A + tan B = 1 - tan A tan B}}
S5. [symbolic] Rearrange the relation -> {{tan A + tan B = 1 - tan A tan B => tan A + tan B + tan A tan B = 1}}
S6. [symbolic] Expand the desired product -> {{(1 + tan A)(1 + tan B) => 1 + tan A + tan B + tan A tan B}}
S7. [symbolic] Substitute the relation from S5 -> {{1 + (tan A + tan B + tan A tan B) => 1 + 1 = 2}}
S8. [symbolic] Replace A and B by 20 degrees and 25 degrees -> {{(1 + tan 20 degrees)(1 + tan 25 degrees) => 2}}

[Check]
V1. Reverse calculation: since tan A + tan B = 1 - tan A tan B, expanding gives 1 + tan A + tan B + tan A tan B = 1 + 1 = 2.

[Answer]
\boxed{2}

